  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Given that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{4x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{\u2033}-8{y}^{\u2032}+16y=0,$ solve the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}-8{y}^{\u2032}+16y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(0)=-1.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, any linear combination of the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$  and ${y}_{2}(x)$  must be a solution to the original differential equation.\n\nHence, the general solution is: \n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{e}^{4x}+{c}_{2}x{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nNow, we find constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=-1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{\u2032}:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=(4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}){e}^{4x}+4{c}_{2}x{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=-1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1=4(1)+{c}_{2}\\,\u27f9\\,{c}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-5x{e}^{4x}.\n$$",
